  1. The Apache License, Version 2.0, reproduced in full below. It grants the
     rights: use, copy, modify, distribute, and a patent license. It also
     carries the clauses on contributions (Section 5) and on trade names
     (Section 6) that this project relies on.

  2. The WinTool Additional Terms, which follow the Apache License below. They
     restrict it on four points: you may not sell WinTool, you must hand the
     source to anyone you hand a modified binary to, you must disclose any data
     collection you add, and you may not pass a fork off as the official
     version.

In one sentence: WinTool is free for everyone, companies included, and nobody
may turn it into something you have to pay for.

Because part 2 restricts part 1, the result is NOT the Apache License 2.0 and
must not be called that. It is also not an Open Source license as the Open
Source Initiative defines the term. That is deliberate, and stated up front
rather than left to be discovered.

Scripts are not part of WinTool. Their authors license them as they wish,
including commercially. See Section 5 of the Additional Terms.

Third-party components keep their own licenses. See THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md.

A French translation of the Additional Terms is provided in LICENSE.fr.md for
convenience. This English text is the one that governs.

